# {{name}}

A modern full-stack web application built with [Veap](https://veap.pl) and Next.js 16 (App Router & React Server Components).

## What is Veap?

**Veap** is a modular application framework built on top of the Next.js App Router. It combines Next.js frontend flexibility with robust, enterprise-grade backend architectural primitives:

- **Clean Architecture & Service Providers**: Inversion of control container and modular service providers configured in `lib/veap.ts`.
- **Virtual Routing**: Dynamic route tree serving installed plugin routes and application routes through `app/[[...catchAll]]/page.tsx` and `app/api/[...catchAll]/route.ts`.
- **Plugin System**: Isolated, composable packages located in `plugins/` with declarative extensions and widgets.
- **ActiveRecord ORM**: Eloquent-style models and query builder backed by Knex with transactions and migrations.
- **Authentication & RBAC**: Built-in session management, user models, role-based access control, and password hashing.
- **Storage & Communication**: Unified file storage drivers and mail transports.

---

## Getting Started

### 1. Requirements

- **Node.js** `>= 22` or **Bun** `>= 1.2`
- Supported package managers: `bun`, `pnpm`, `npm`, or `yarn`

### 2. Environment Configuration

During project initialization, a default `.env` file is generated with a secure random `ENCRYPTION_KEY` and a local SQLite database configuration:

```bash
DATABASE_CLIENT=sqlite3
DATABASE_URL=./storage/veap.sqlite
ENCRYPTION_KEY=<generated-key>
```

> **Important**: The application enforces fail-fast verification on `ENCRYPTION_KEY`. Never commit `.env` to version control.

### 3. Run the Development Server

Start the local development server:

```bash
# Using bun
bun dev

# Or using pnpm
pnpm dev

# Or using npm
npm run dev
```

Open [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000) with your browser to view the application.

---

## Available Scripts

| Command | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `bun dev` | Starts the Next.js development server with Turbopack |
| `bun run build` | Builds the application for production |
| `bun run start` | Runs the production build |
| `bun run lint` | Runs ESLint checks |

*(Replace `bun` with `pnpm` or `npm run` depending on your package manager)*

---

## Veap CLI

The `veap` CLI manages plugins, database migrations, and framework components:

```bash
# Add and register a plugin package
veap add <plugin-name>

# Scaffold a new workspace plugin in plugins/
veap make:plugin <plugin-name>

# Create a new database migration
veap make:migration <migration-name>

# Scaffold a new site layout template
veap make:template <template-name>

# Re-synchronize and generate lib/plugins.gen.ts
veap register

# Generate Docker deployment configuration
veap docker
```

---

## Project Structure

```
├── app/
│   ├── (site)/             # Declarative site layout route group
│   ├── [[...catchAll]]/    # Veap virtual page router
│   ├── api/[...catchAll]/  # Veap API pipeline (middlewares, RBAC, auth)
│   ├── storage/            # Local storage route handler
│   ├── layout.tsx          # Root layout shell with force-dynamic bootstrap
│   └── globals.css         # Tailwind CSS styling
├── components/             # Reusable UI components (SiteLayout, Navbar, Footer)
├── lib/
│   ├── veap.ts             # Composition root: Application configuration & bootstrap
│   └── plugins.gen.ts      # Generated plugin registry (managed by veap register)
├── migrations/             # Database migrations
├── plugins/                # Local workspace plugins
├── storage/                # Local SQLite database & uploaded files
├── AGENTS.md               # AI coding agent instructions and architectural contracts
└── next.config.ts          # Next.js configuration with Veap & Turbopack support
```

